    <name>
      <text>Area Between Curves-Q2</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Determine the area of the region bounded by \({@expr1@}, \, {@expr2@}, \, y={@y_1@}\) and \(y={@y_2@}\)</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>

<p><b>Recall:</b> Area between two curves with respect to the y-axis.</p>
<p>If \(f(y)\) and \(g(y)\) are continuous on \([a, b]\) and \(f(y) &lt; g(y)\) for all \(y\) in \([a, b]\), then</p>
<p>Area \(= \displaystyle \int_{a}^{b} [g(y) -f(y)] dy\)</p>
<p>In other words if the area of the region bounded by \(x=f(y), \, x= g(y), \, y=a \, \text{and} \,y=b\) exists then the area is given by
</p>
<p>Area \(= \displaystyle \int_{a}^{b} | f(y) -g(y) | dy\) where  \( f(y)= {@expr1_1@}, \, g(y)= {@expr2_1@}, \, a={@y_1@}\) and \(b={@y_2@}\)</p>
<p>Therefore,</p>
<p>
\(
\begin{align}
A &amp;= \displaystyle \int_{{@y_1@}}^{{@y_2@}} \left( {@rhs(expr1)@}-({@rhs(expr2)@}) \right) dy \\
   &amp;= \displaystyle \int_{{@y_1@}}^{{@y_2@}} \left(&nbsp;{@(rhs(expr1)-rhs(expr2))@}\right) dy \\
   &amp;= \displaystyle \left[{@area_func@}\right]_{{@y_1@}}^{{@y_2@}} \\
   &amp;= {@ta@}

\end{align}
\)
</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
